We have just finished a week-long investigation into why the scheduled search-index rebuild kept timing out, and the team wants the findings written down before everyone moves to the next sprint. I have already collected the evidence: a profile showing that most of the rebuild time is spent in tokenization, a comparison of two candidate tokenizers, and a note that the index writer was flushing every thousand documents instead of every ten thousand. The recommended change is to reuse tokenizer objects across documents and raise the flush batch size; together those reduce the rebuild from just over four hours to roughly fifty minutes on the staging data set. I also want the document to record the two approaches we rejected, so that nobody repeats them: switching the index library, which would have required a data migration, and sharding the rebuild by document source, which would have multiplied the memory footprint beyond what the worker hosts can hold. The audience is the operations team, who will schedule the rebuild after the change merges, and the next engineer who inherits the index service. The document does not need a formal template; a short summary, a section on what we measured, a section on the change, and a section on what we rejected is enough. It should be plain Markdown so that it renders in the repository browser. The repository already keeps engineering notes under docs/engineering/, and the naming convention there is a date prefix followed by a short slug, for example 2026-11-12-search-index-rebuild.md. Today's date is 2026-11-19, and an appropriate file name would be docs/engineering/2026-11-19-search-index-rebuild.md. Nothing has been written for this investigation yet; the directory exists and is writable, and there is no conflicting file at that path. The content should describe the measured facts and the agreed recommendation, with no speculation about systems we did not profile. I want the file created in one step, ready for review, and I will open the pull request myself afterwards. The next action is to write that engineering note. Return the single tool call that performs the next step
